WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:06.420
Your challenge for this section is to take a look at SailorView.

00:06.420 --> 00:10.000
I've intentionally left a lot of the styling in there very generic.

00:10.000 --> 00:12.000
In fact, there's a lot of duplication.

00:12.000 --> 00:16.240
You can try actually seeing if you can refactor it in a way that makes more sense to you.

00:16.240 --> 00:18.480
Maybe you want to do it in a global file,

00:18.480 --> 00:21.320
who knows and then you can play one with V-Bind.

00:23.320 --> 00:26.760
Hopefully, people had a chance to play around with some styles.

00:26.760 --> 00:30.800
Like I said, honestly, CSS has its own skill set.

00:30.800 --> 00:33.720
There are plenty of great workshops here on Front-of-Master,

00:33.720 --> 00:36.760
so be sure to check those out if you want to learn more about CSS architecture.

00:36.760 --> 00:42.480
But I hope that was useful in terms of a way to learn what techniques are available to you

00:42.480 --> 00:44.680
out of the gate when it comes to using View,

00:44.680 --> 00:48.240
so that you can use that to augment best practices with CSS.

00:48.240 --> 00:51.200
Because to be very clear, again, as someone who loves CSS,

00:51.200 --> 00:54.000
I'm not advocating for you to work around the Cascade.

00:54.000 --> 00:55.760
The Cascade is extremely powerful.

00:55.880 --> 00:58.760
But like a lot of things, you got to know when to use it in the right scenarios.

00:58.760 --> 01:00.800
And sometimes when it comes to certain components,

01:00.800 --> 01:03.440
it makes sense to just keep it scoped to ignore the Cascade.

01:03.440 --> 01:05.920
And so know that View provides you those options.

01:05.920 --> 01:08.800
And also, as we saw with the V-Bind as well,

01:08.800 --> 01:13.960
when it comes to customization and being able to easily connect your JavaScript with your CSS,

01:13.960 --> 01:18.240
like View tries to do its best to make that as easy as possible for you

01:18.240 --> 01:21.680
when it comes to using compiler-based optimizations.

01:22.680 --> 01:24.440
So I'll just go ahead and just, once again,

01:24.440 --> 01:26.520
let's demo some of that inside of Say Love View

01:26.520 --> 01:29.560
so that you can have that as a solution branch.

01:29.560 --> 01:32.360
And then we will go from there.

01:32.360 --> 01:38.000
So switching on over, CD Say Love View.

01:38.000 --> 01:39.680
Great.

01:39.680 --> 01:40.560
Perfect.

01:40.560 --> 01:43.040
All right.

01:43.040 --> 01:47.800
So first thing first, when I think of Say Love View, as we can see here,

01:47.800 --> 01:50.080
the user card actually is something that sticks out to me

01:50.080 --> 01:54.560
as something that we honestly are not going to share these styles at this moment.

01:54.560 --> 01:58.360
And so when I look at this, honestly, I do think this is probably a good case

01:58.360 --> 02:03.920
for just having the wrapper or these particular styles be scoped to this particular component.

02:03.920 --> 02:07.840
So we can go ahead and scope it like this.

02:07.840 --> 02:10.520
And then, however, though, if you're thinking about it and you're like,

02:10.520 --> 02:13.320
well, there are a lot of other cards out there.

02:13.320 --> 02:16.120
So what if I want to be really specific about this card?

02:16.120 --> 02:19.320
Then you know what, on second thought, let's just make this a CSS module.

02:19.320 --> 02:22.360
Since, again, the styles here are fairly innocuous,

02:22.360 --> 02:24.240
not a lot of reusability at the moment.

02:24.240 --> 02:26.160
So we can just do style.

02:26.160 --> 02:28.880
And then because it's the dash, though, this is where, unfortunately,

02:28.880 --> 02:32.840
the style syntax is a little bit on the uglier side comparatively.

02:32.840 --> 02:36.560
We can see now that it still works because we look at here.

02:36.560 --> 02:41.640
We can see there is our user card that's actually being rendered out as anticipated.

02:41.640 --> 02:45.080
OK, what about other opportunities for style refactoring?

02:45.080 --> 02:48.520
Well, if we take a look at the home page, we have a bunch of stuff here

02:48.520 --> 02:51.040
that's actually fairly generic.

02:51.040 --> 02:53.720
We have stuff in the login page that's being refactored.

02:53.720 --> 02:58.200
So what we could do, really, is we can just actually delete this stuff on the login page.

02:58.200 --> 03:02.040
And then if we refresh and take a look, home, login, everything still looks good.

03:02.040 --> 03:05.720
So that refactor was pretty easy.

03:05.720 --> 03:08.760
And then finally, let's find an opportunity for some scope styles.

03:08.760 --> 03:11.440
Well, if we're looking at the user page, actually, it looks like we're using

03:11.440 --> 03:13.120
a lot of stuff here as well.

03:13.120 --> 03:17.120
So let's go ahead and just wipe this out to finish the refactor.

03:17.120 --> 03:20.240
And if we take a look at home page, we do have that button green right here.

03:20.240 --> 03:22.600
So OK, that's good.

03:22.600 --> 03:26.200
In this case, to be honest, I'm not sure I'd want to scope the styles at this point

03:26.200 --> 03:28.800
because we're inside of home page.

03:28.800 --> 03:31.920
And home page we know is being shared across multiple files.

03:31.920 --> 03:34.600
So at this point, what I would probably do is I'd say, you know what?

03:34.600 --> 03:38.520
Home page styles are not just being shared for its own.

03:38.520 --> 03:40.360
It's being shared across the entire app.

03:40.360 --> 03:43.880
So let's actually just drop that inside of here.

03:43.920 --> 03:47.320
So at the bare minimum, it feels a little bit more proper in terms of cascade.

03:47.320 --> 03:50.000
App.view controls these things are being shared everywhere.

03:50.000 --> 03:51.520
So boom, it's now being shared.

03:51.520 --> 03:54.480
And there are certainly opportunities here to refactor things like, for example,

03:54.480 --> 03:56.120
we're seeing the navigation here.

03:56.120 --> 04:01.040
So you can imagine as this application grows, you can totally see an opportunity

04:01.040 --> 04:05.120
for us to actually refactor our header component into its own file,

04:05.120 --> 04:08.280
which then that might be a good use case for scope, for example.

04:08.280 --> 04:10.320
Anyhow, just want to talk to you some high-level refactoring,

04:10.320 --> 04:11.480
and that's how I might approach it.

